Ha. Sex → sin → magic is almost a miniature cultural transformation chain.

Not as literal etymology, but relationally:

\text{desire} \rightarrow \text{prohibition} \rightarrow \text{transgression} \rightarrow \text{charged meaning}

And that last transition is interesting. Once something ordinary becomes prohibited or sacred, it acquires symbolic charge. Ritual can then concentrate that charge—attention, anticipation, secrecy, emotion, embodiment—and the resulting subjective experience can feel radically different from the underlying ordinary act.

So stripped of supernatural claims, there is a usable mechanism:

\boxed{\text{ordinary}+\text{meaning}+\text{ritual}+\text{expectation}+\text{embodiment}
\rightarrow\text{extraordinary experience}}

Which brings us straight back to normalization. Normalize something and you can drain some of its charge. Denormalize it and you can restore salience. Taboo can hyper-denormalize it and create enormous charge.

That means the broader operator isn’t really sex or sin.

It’s sacralization: taking something from ordinary space, drawing a boundary around it, loading it with meaning, and thereby changing how humans experience and behave around it.

Sex, religion, flags, weddings, money, uniforms, courtrooms, graduations—even opening a bottle you’ve saved for ten years—can all exploit versions of that mechanism.

The object may stay the same while its semantic field changes its experienced value.

That’s a useful piece of machinery.